import{_ as s,o as t,c as i,a3 as a}from"./chunks/framework.Ch_0qWu9.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Solver judge isolation","description":"","frontmatter":{"sourcePath":"docs/judge-execution-isolation.md","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/MoonshotAI/TCSExam/blob/29e5ae6c70cf7268cd38de5cf59f4e08507b1ff6/docs/judge-execution-isolation.md","alternateUrl":"/TCSExam/docs/zh/judge-execution-isolation/","alternateAnchors":{"solver-judge-isolation":"solver-judge-isolation","check-the-execution-host":"check-the-execution-host","privileges-file-access-and-cleanup":"privileges-file-access-and-cleanup"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"judge-execution-isolation/index.md","filePath":"judge-execution-isolation/index.md"}'),r={name:"judge-execution-isolation/index.md"};function n(o,e,c,d,l,p){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a(`<h1 id="solver-judge-isolation" tabindex="-1">Solver judge isolation <a class="header-anchor" href="#solver-judge-isolation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Solver judge isolation&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This document describes process privileges, network access, filesystem writes, and cleanup in Solver judges. It is intended for maintainers of Online and Offline judge images and backend services.</p><h2 id="check-the-execution-host" tabindex="-1">Check the execution host <a class="header-anchor" href="#check-the-execution-host" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Check the execution host&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The judge requires Linux x86-64, root supervision, seccomp filters, and an enabled Landlock ABI of at least 3, including truncate protection. The preflight checks the image&#39;s user/group transitions and actual syscall policy. Worker startup runs a bounded enforcement preflight before opening its HTTP listener; Offline workers run the same check before judging. A failed check exits at startup and is reported through the existing judge boot failure path. Resolve the reported requirement before retrying.</p><p>To check a prepared judge image on the actual Docker host, run:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">docker</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> run</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --rm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --network</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> none</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --entrypoint</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> python3</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> JUDGE_IMAGE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">  /judge/process_supervisor.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> preflight</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Success prints JSON containing <code>ok</code>, <code>landlock_abi</code>, uid/gid, groups, <code>network_denied</code>, and <code>descendant_network_denied</code>. The probe actually drops privileges, installs a policy, writes an allowed output, and checks that a world-writable protected sentinel cannot be changed. It also checks socket denial in both the submission process and an executed child, while permitting private Unix socket pairs. An unsupported kernel, disabled Landlock LSM, denied syscall, or unavailable identity transition fails. Changing the container&#39;s userspace does not add missing host kernel support. Landlock must be enabled in both the kernel build and boot configuration; see the <a href="https://docs.kernel.org/userspace-api/landlock.html#kernel-support" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Linux kernel documentation</a>. Container seccomp policies must permit the three Landlock syscalls. Run the same preflight after changing the host or its security profile.</p><p>Run the preflight on the same host and image that will execute judging. After it passes, use the normal task workflow to execute submissions.</p><h2 id="privileges-file-access-and-cleanup" tabindex="-1">Privileges, file access and cleanup <a class="header-anchor" href="#privileges-file-access-and-cleanup" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Privileges, file access and cleanup&quot;">​</a></h2><p>All 30 Solver tasks share <code>assets/environments/solver/judge-runtime/execution.py</code>. Online judges install it through <code>run_checker.py</code>; the common Offline worker and task-specific Offline workers use the same boundary. The scoring layer defines the instances, reference values, time limits and shared time pools.</p><p>Build and solve commands run as uid/gid 65534 with no supplementary groups. Trusted validators and input preparation retain the checker identity. The adapter classifies calls from the audited build/solve functions; an unclassified scoring subprocess fails explicitly. <code>test_judge_execution.py</code> checks the scoring implementations&#39; subprocess classifications.</p><p>Untrusted commands use <code>no_new_privs</code> to prevent privilege gains through setuid executables or file capabilities. Their environment contains a fixed executable search path, workspace and temporary-directory settings, locale, numeric budgets, and required output paths. The search path includes the trusted MiniZinc and graph tool directories declared by the development images; arbitrary inherited PATH, loader settings, Python search paths, and service credentials are excluded.</p><p>An inherited seccomp filter blocks socket creation and connection, listening, and <code>io_uring</code> in build and solve processes. This covers Internet, loopback and Unix-domain service sockets. Private Unix socket pairs, pipes, threads and child processes remain available for local computation. The supervisor closes inherited file descriptors apart from standard input, output and error before executing the command. Filter installation failure stops the command.</p><p>The trusted worker service and its tunnel keep their network access to receive submissions and return results. Harbor&#39;s development-container allowlist remains configured separately; Online Docker workers are created by the backend adapter and enforce submission network isolation through this shared runtime.</p><p>Landlock restricts filesystem writes to the build directory, <code>/app</code>, explicit output files or directories, and a private temporary directory for each command. An input sharing a directory with an output file remains protected. JSSP uses a separate <code>out-&lt;instance&gt;</code> output directory; that directory is writable so its documented temporary-file-and-rename output protocol continues to work. The shipped QBF wrapper&#39;s explicit <code>mktemp /tmp/...</code> template is redirected into its private TMPDIR. Other temporary writes should use TMPDIR or the workspace.</p><p>Submission archives reject symlinks, hard links, and special files. Builds may create symlinks within the build directory as needed by compilation tools; links escaping the build directory are removed before returning to the checker. Solve commands cannot create symlinks, and trusted Python writes reject symlink traversal. A separate compilation directory returns to root ownership with group/other write permission removed before inputs can be materialized there.</p><p>Each submission and each untrusted command has its own Linux subreaper supervisor. It cleans up descendants even after <code>setsid</code> or a double fork. A scorer&#39;s SIGTERM is forwarded to the candidate process group and preserves the scorer&#39;s original grace period. The scorer&#39;s subsequent SIGKILL uses a separate supervisor control signal to request immediate descendant cleanup. Normal exits and exceptions also clean up background descendants. Cleanup targets only this supervisor&#39;s children.</p><p>Cleanup has a five-second deadline. A failure writes a trusted execution-error marker. The Online worker observes that marker while waiting, records a <code>judge_error</code>, stops its queue, rejects new submissions, and returns an unhealthy status. Its bounded shutdown wait does not permit reuse after an unresolved cleanup. <code>/submissions</code> retains completed and failed records and reports the worker error; the backend archives those records and invokes its existing failed shard teardown, which stops the actual worker. Offline workers exit unsuccessfully and their controller removes the container. Cleanup failures count as judging infrastructure failures rather than candidate build failures or timeouts.</p>`,18)])])}const k=s(r,[["render",n]]);export{h as __pageData,k as default};
